WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.100
We're going to figure 
something out, damn it!

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.160
<i>All right, I'm doing this.</i>

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.640
What were you shouting 
about all of a sudden?!

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.710
Sorry. I just thought 
it'd help me get motivated.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.440
Oh, I see!

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.320
You're going to solve this 
like you usually do, then?!

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.940
That's a huge relief!

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.100
You don't mind if I handle 
the response, then?

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.610
Th-That's fine.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.270
But there are some points 
that aren't negotiable, understand?

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.930
I do.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.150
To start things off, we need 
to swap engines for MysClo right away.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.390
What are you talking about?!

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.830
What's the problem?

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.420
The majority of the bugs are rooted directly
 in the internally-developed engine.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.160
Then just have them fixed!

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.650
Kawasegawa-kun was just talking 
about how that should suffice!

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:31.090
I'm pretty sure she said that reluctantly, 
because of how adamant you were.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.840
But in terms of what the team is encountering, 
it's frankly too far gone to fix.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.090
I-Is that the truth, Kawasegawa?

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:41.450
Yes.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.960
But changing the engine 
at this point can't be done, can it?!

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.680
There's no time! How would 
we make a new one?!

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.530
We already have an engine.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.940
Sorry about the holdup!

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.420
Go ahead and get started.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.960
Leave it to me. I'll get things 
going in no time.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.700
Share your screens with mine, please.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.710
This is the executable for MysClo.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.370
I'll just drag it over here...

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.130
Wait, what? It doesn't need 
to be tuned or anything?

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.360
It's fine.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.800
Then we just run the executable, and...

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.960
Hey, he's right! It started up 
with no extra work!

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.690
And it's not chewing up resources, either.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.310
Or crashing from a bug.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.230
What exactly did you do?

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.720
It was easy.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.230
That's the Enidy engine, which is
 famous for how stable it is.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.320
E-Enidy?!

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.760
That thing costs a fortune! 
And you ordered it without asking?!

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.580
This is a huge issue!

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.680
It would be, so I used Team B's budget.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.960
That's misappropriation!

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.960
What exactly do you think 
you're doing, as team lead—

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.060
I did use Team B's budget,

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.010
but why wouldn't I, considering that's 
what the funds were budgeted for?

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.380
I get it.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.080
You chose an engine 
that'd also be compatible

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.890
with Team A's game, didn't you?

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.020
Sharp as always. You got it.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.460
What are you talking about?!

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.370
That has to break the terms of the license!

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.870
We'd need to spend a ton
 on the additional licenses—

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.840
What's this?!

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.810
An estimate on the cost 
of the additional licenses,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.680
and Team A's total production budget.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.450
Even with the extra cost, 
you'll still have plenty left over.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.250
Were you after this the entire time?

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.170
I like to be prepared for the worst.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.160
Ideally it wouldn't have come to this, 
but we're past that point.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.800
Okay, let's figure out 
the official response.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.730
Producer Kawasegawa 
and I already talked this over.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:46.800
Uh, Hashiba—

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.190
It's okay.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.910
First of all, for the person in charge 
of our social media accounts...

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.320
Morishita-san, that's you.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.730
Wait, me?!

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.570
You've got the broadest 
outlook of everyone here,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.850
which puts you closest 
to how the players would feel.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.190
You're the best choice.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.210
But I'm not really good 
at writing official-sounding copy...

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.080
And what'll I do if I make the users mad?

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.110
There are times when that kind of response

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.620
is more helpful than 
rock-solid business speak.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.540
O-Okay then!

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.140
To start with, could you contact the agency 
of that voice actor, from the incident?

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.760
Are you talking about Suenomori-san?

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:23.870
Yes.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.230
We'll apologize on the phone and via mail,

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:30.920
and make it clear, publicly, 
that they did nothing wrong.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.150
Plus, let them know we'll be 
handing over the casting decisions

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.640
for new characters appearing 
in the foreseeable future to them.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.770
Wait, you can't just hand over 
the casting rights like that!

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:41.820
Do you realize how valuable—

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.020
That value is why we need to hand it over.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.570
We'd be looking at a bigger loss 
if this turns into a defamation suit

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.660
with a settlement involved.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.360
Okay, next... Who's in charge of the scripting?

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.650
That's me.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:59.420
How long do you think it'd take for 
a comprehensive check of the systems?

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.750
I can't say definitively...

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:05.170
But when you throw in all of the 
nitty-gritty testing, I think around six days?

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.620
Six days?!

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.180
You've got three! Figure it out in three—

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.860
Okay, you've got two weeks. 
Make sure to take care of it.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.340
Are you insane?! We can't 
suspend the service for two—

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.550
When you consider it's 
only ten business days,

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.240
that leaves five days 
for adjustments, three for testing,

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.680
and two for implementation.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.850
How's that sound?

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.230
That's more than enough, yeah!

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:24.670
Okay, next...

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.980
How many apology gems 
do we normally hand out?

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.190
For a project like this?

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.910
Usually enough for two or three 
gacha ten-pulls is appropriate.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.600
In that case, let's do ten times that.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:39.920
D-Did you say ten?!

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.770
I've never heard of a game 
that hands out gems like that!

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.840
Enough for one extra should be fine!

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.580
If we did that, the problems we've had 
would far outshine anything else.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.010
We need a big enough 
response to get people talking.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.140
And since this part is the most important...

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.680
I want you all to listen to me very closely.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.640
Everybody is off for the 
next two days. Period.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:03.110
No working at all. Okay?

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.050
Time off?!

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:09.290
What the hell would be the point of having 
time off when everything is on fire?!

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.470
We'll implement our initial 
social media response first, of course.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:15.280
If we publicize our plans beforehand, 
it shouldn't be a problem.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.200
What are you going on about?!

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.710
This is a massive project that affects 
the fate of the entire company!

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.530
Do you understand that?!

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:27.720
I wouldn't have come up with this plan 
if I didn't understand it, sir.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.270
It doesn't seem like you do!

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:33.340
Nothing you've suggested 
is going to profit the company!

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.430
Do you hear me?! In business, 
unless you bring in profits—

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.320
President!

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:45.100
You mentioned once 
that you trust me, remember?

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.020
Y-Yeah...

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.730
There was something else you said.

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.610
About how this was an important time 
for trust in the company.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.120
I think you understand just 
how important trust can be.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.380
And I won't deny that meeting deadlines 
is an important part of maintaining trust.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:07.680
I'm sure everyone here regrets 
the fact that wasn't possible.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.190
R-Right?!

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.010
Which is why the sooner 
the service is functional again—

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:16.200
That said, the best way to recover 
trust after such a huge mistake

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.510
isn't just getting the service 
back up and running.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.120
We need to demonstrate how we're aware 
of our culpability in a tangible fashion,

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:26.730
and make sure our future plans 
are as clear as possible.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.190
Right now, this game has made 
the worst possible first impression.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.930
Which is why we need to go above and beyond,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.200
or no one will come back to it.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:40.700
Was the reaction to MysClo really that bad?

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:47.710
There's a story about an app 
that developed a critical bug.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.690
The marketing team 
was trying to cover it up,

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:54.740
but the person at the core of development 
decided to go public with it,

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.220
without making any excuses.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:59.720
Why do you know about that?

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.910
An old friend of yours told me about it.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:04.940
Because you were honest about the problems,

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:08.060
in the end, people trusted that app 
more than they would have.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.360
That was ages ago. A youthful misadventure.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.190
Really?

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.390
Because I don't think being 
honest and apologetic

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.370
could possibly be a bad thing.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.740
I understand everything 
you're trying to say, sir.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.820
But still...

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.440
Maybe you should rethink 
what's really important,

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.750
especially in an emergency like this.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.260
You don't have to dedicate 
everything to it. But please...

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:37.440
Think a little about 
what's going on around here.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:46.330
Fine. You're free to deal 
with this as you see fit.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:48.920
What a relief!

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.810
Maybe now we can salvage this thing!

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:53.230
I'm gonna go ahead 
and start prepping for testing.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.230
And then let's finally go home!

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.320
You had better go home, too. 
It'd defeat the purpose, otherwise.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.650
Oh, don't worry, you don't have to tell me.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:06.300
I could seriously use a bath 
and some sleep.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:08.790
Hashiba...

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:11.600
Thank you.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:14.880
You're very welcome.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.430
Excuse me, sir.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.930
Thank you for being so understanding.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:29.190
Did you make that proposal knowing I'd fold?

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.940
No. To be honest, 
I didn't know how you'd react.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:35.480
I guess you're quite the gambler, then.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.940
I wouldn't call it that. It's just...

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:41.200
I was serious.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.660
And I couldn't come up 
with any other answers.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.230
That was your "serious," huh?

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.280
No wonder it was such a strong argument.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:52.260
I want to make you a guarantee 
in exchange for your understanding.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.050
What guarantee?

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.130
And that guarantee...

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.580
Was getting me to turn in 
my illustrations, right?

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:04.550
Yes.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:08.440
I would love to help, but...

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:12.870
Sensei, why not really try 
and get back to your roots?

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:13.980
My roots?

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.400
I think that the hint to your breakthrough

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.200
has something to do 
with why you started drawing.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.270
Are these...

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.370
It's the package illustration for HaruSora.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.650
But... how?

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.840
I never actually told you...

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.020
Right.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.840
But when I saw the picture you working 
on the other day, I was sure of it.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:45.950
"I know art like this. Very intimately."

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:53.350
I was thinking about quitting 
art once in high school.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.960
What made you think that?

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.470
"Your roughs and color 
composition are well done,"

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:03.660
"but your art is pure technical ability." 
A teacher said that to me.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.990
That my art had no real charm.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:10.600
That got me depressed, 
and I started hating art.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:17.250
It was around then that a game-loving 
senpai of mine got me into HaruSora.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:18.910
I was blown away!

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:22.440
I realized just how amazing art 
with charm could be!

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:25.880
I felt just how much it could move somebody!

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:30.060
And I started to want to make art like that, 
from the very bottom of my heart.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:35.670
And, some day, I wanted to draw with 
the person who was responsible for that art.

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.270
But she's already...

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:39.610
I know.

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:43.940
That's why I couldn't bring myself 
to tell you, Hashiba-san.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.230
I'm sorry. It's my fault.

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:48.040
Please, don't apologize!

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:53.040
I mean... it ended up 
helping me again, y'know?

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.540
That makes the second time 
this art has done that.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:58.830
Give me a week to turn in some rough drafts!

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:02.260
Another few days after that 
for the line art and colors!

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:04.510
Sensei!

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:07.570
I'm going to draw! I actually want to!

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:15.100
<b><i>\h\h\hCharacter Design
Minori Ayaka</i></b>

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:18.650
Hashiba-san!

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:22.610
Ayaka-sensei just called about 
wanting to do another character!

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:23.900
That's great to hear!

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.950
It looks like the survey 
from last week is out, too!

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:27.910
Yeah.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:31.500
The system's stabilized, the new 
characters are coming together nicely...

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.270
I think we've managed 
to recover from the disaster.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.790
Although things are only going to 
get more busy around here.

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:38.410
It's okay!

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:43.940
That's why Kawasegawa-san came up 
with that super-thick manual!

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:46.300
That way things can keep running 
even when the leader's absent.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.180
She thought of everything, huh?

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:52.650
Everyone's talking about 
how amazing you were, though.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.850
I don't know if anyone else 
would've been capable

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:56.940
of turning things around 
in a situation like that!

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.760
It wasn't such a big deal.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.110
I can't really do anything...

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.200
Oh, it's lunch time.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:05.530
Maybe I'll invite Kawasegawa to join—

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:08.070
Oh, she didn't tell you, Hashiba-san?

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:08.860
Tell me what?

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:12.600
The producer's got a flight 
leaving Haneda at 13:00.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.300
She said she was going to Okinawa.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:17.610
Morishita-san, tell everyone 
I'm taking the afternoon off!

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.210
Hashiba-san, wait—

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.560
<i>I did it. I did it again!</i>

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:30.750
<i>I just bulldozed over her in a situation 
where she was in charge.</i>

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.580
<i>It's obvious that would put her 
in an awkward position...</i>

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.220
<i>Costing her the trust of her subordinates.</i>

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:39.750
<i>I haven't learned a single thing 
after what happened to Tsurayuki.</i>

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.810
How could I be so stupid?!

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.010
<i>I'm sorry, Kawasegawa.</i>

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.470
<i>I couldn't help you a second time.</i>

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:06.030
Wait... What are you doing here?

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.700
Kawasegawa...

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Departure Time</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Destination</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Okinawa / Naha</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Sapporo / Shin Chitose</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Osaka / Kansai</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Kushiro</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Memanbetsu</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Hakodate</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Tokachi-Obihiro</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Aomori</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>Flight No.</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>First Class</b>

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:14.510
<b>J Class</b>

